# Convergence of $\sum_{n=3}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n(\log n)^{(\log\log n)^q}}$

**Answer check first:** The problem text contains no leaked solution, answer, or proof sketch. Proceeding.

## Setup

For $n$ sufficiently large, $\log n > 1$ and $\log\log n > 0$, so the summand
$$f(n) = \frac{1}{n(\log n)^{(\log\log n)^q}}$$
is positive and (eventually) decreasing. By the **integral test**, the series converges if and only if
$$I = \int_{3}^{\infty} \frac{dx}{x\,(\log x)^{(\log\log x)^q}}$$
converges.

## Reduction by substitution

**First substitution:** let $u = \log x$, so $du = dx/x$. Then
$$I = \int_{\log 3}^{\infty} \frac{du}{u^{(\log u)^q}}.$$

**Second substitution:** let $v = \log u$, so $u = e^v$ and $du = e^v\,dv$. Since $(\log u)^q = v^q$ and
$$u^{(\log u)^q} = (e^v)^{v^q} = e^{v\cdot v^q} = e^{v^{q+1}},$$
we obtain
$$I = \int_{\log\log 3}^{\infty} \frac{e^v\,dv}{e^{v^{q+1}}} = \int_{\log\log 3}^{\infty} \exp\!\bigl(v - v^{q+1}\bigr)\,dv.$$

So convergence of the original series is equivalent to convergence of
$$\int_{A}^{\infty} e^{\,v - v^{q+1}}\,dv, \qquad A = \log\log 3.$$

## Case analysis on $q$

The asymptotic behavior of the exponent $\varphi(v) = v - v^{q+1}$ as $v\to\infty$ determines everything.

**Case $q > 0$ (i.e. $q+1 > 1$).** Then $v^{q+1}$ grows strictly faster than $v$, so $\varphi(v) = v - v^{q+1} \to -\infty$. More precisely, for large $v$, $v^{q+1} \geq 2v$, hence $\varphi(v) \leq -v$ and
$$e^{\varphi(v)} \leq e^{-v}.$$
Since $\int_A^\infty e^{-v}\,dv$ converges, $I$ converges by comparison. **Series converges.**

**Case $q = 0$ (i.e. $q+1 = 1$).** Then $\varphi(v) = v - v = 0$, so the integrand is identically $e^0 = 1$, and
$$I = \int_A^\infty 1\,dv = +\infty.$$
**Series diverges.** (Equivalently, the original series reduces to $\sum \frac{1}{n\log n}$, the classical divergent Bertrand series.)

**Case $-1 < q < 0$ (i.e. $0 < q+1 < 1$).** Then $v^{q+1} = o(v)$, so $\varphi(v) = v - v^{q+1} \to +\infty$. In particular $\varphi(v) \geq v/2$ for large $v$, giving $e^{\varphi(v)} \geq e^{v/2} \to \infty$. The integrand does not even tend to $0$. **Series diverges.**

**Case $q = -1$ (i.e. $q+1 = 0$).** Then $v^{q+1} = v^0 = 1$, so $\varphi(v) = v - 1 \to +\infty$ and $e^{\varphi(v)} = e^{v-1} \to \infty$. **Series diverges.**

**Case $q < -1$ (i.e. $q+1 < 0$).** Then $v^{q+1} \to 0$ as $v\to\infty$, so $\varphi(v) = v - v^{q+1} \to +\infty$ and $e^{\varphi(v)} \to \infty$. **Series diverges.**

## Conclusion

The series converges if and only if $q > 0$.

$$\boxed{q \in (0,\,+\infty)}$$

